CAT = "ukcarePerson"; SEG = "PCW"
# Standard 5 Work in a person-centred way (incl. mental capacity), Standard 7 Privacy and dignity
Q = [
("What is person-centred care?",
 "Care built around the person", ["Care planned around the staff rota", "Care that is the same for everyone", "Care decided mainly by the family"],
 "Person-centred care puts the individual at the centre, with care shaped by their needs, preferences, history and choices."),
("Which of these is a person-centred value?",
 "Choice", ["Speed", "Routine", "Uniformity"],
 "Person-centred values include individuality, independence, privacy, partnership, choice, dignity, respect and rights."),
("Where should you find out how a person likes to be supported?",
 "Their care plan and the person", ["The staff notice board", "The handover sheet from last week", "The visitors' book at reception"],
 "The care plan records the person's needs and preferences, and the person themselves is the best source of what they want today."),
("A person who usually has a shower says they want a wash at the sink today. What should you do?",
 "Respect the choice and record it", ["Insist on the shower as planned", "Skip personal care altogether", "Ask their family to persuade them"],
 "People can change their minds. Respect the choice, support the wash and record it; report any pattern that may affect health."),
("Which law sets out how to support people who may lack mental capacity in England and Wales?",
 "The Mental Capacity Act 2005", ["The Mental Health Act 1983", "The Equality Act 2010", "The Human Rights Act 1998"],
 "The Mental Capacity Act 2005 sets out how to assess capacity and make decisions in a person's best interests. The Mental Health Act covers assessment and treatment of mental disorder, including detention."),
("What is the first principle of the Mental Capacity Act 2005?",
 "Assume a person has capacity", ["Act in the family's interests", "Assume older people lack capacity", "Always choose the safest option"],
 "A person must be assumed to have capacity unless it is established that they lack it. Age, appearance or diagnosis alone never prove a lack of capacity."),
("Under the Mental Capacity Act, a person makes a choice that others think is unwise. What does this mean?",
 "It does not prove they lack capacity", ["They must lack capacity for that choice", "Staff should overrule the decision", "Their family takes over the decision"],
 "One of the five principles is that a person is not to be treated as unable to make a decision merely because it is unwise."),
("Capacity is assessed for what?",
 "One decision at one time", ["All decisions for the rest of life", "All decisions for the next month", "Only decisions about money"],
 "Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific. A person may be able to choose what to wear but not to manage complex finances, and capacity can change."),
("Which of these is part of the Mental Capacity Act's test of whether a person can make a decision?",
 "Can they retain the information?", ["Can they read and write in English?", "Do they agree with staff advice?", "Have they been to school?"],
 "A person is unable to decide if they cannot understand the relevant information, retain it, use or weigh it, or communicate their decision."),
("If a decision is made for a person who lacks capacity, how must it be made?",
 "In their best interests", ["By the most senior relative", "In the service's best interests", "By the quickest method"],
 "Decisions made for a person who lacks capacity must be in their best interests and should be the option least restrictive of their rights and freedom."),
("What can a lasting power of attorney do?",
 "Let a chosen person decide for them", ["Let staff decide all of the care", "Let a doctor manage the person's money", "Let the council sell the person's home"],
 "A lasting power of attorney is made while a person has capacity. It names who can make health and welfare or property and financial decisions if they later lose capacity."),
("What should you do before giving personal care?",
 "Explain and get the person's consent", ["Start quickly to save them time", "Ask a colleague to give consent", "Ask the family for consent each time"],
 "Valid consent is needed before any care or support. Explain what you plan to do in a way the person understands and check they agree."),
("A person refuses to take part in an activity they usually enjoy. What should you do?",
 "Respect it and gently ask why", ["Take them along anyway", "Record them as uncooperative", "Remove the activity from the plan"],
 "People have the right to refuse. Respect the decision and gently explore the reason, which may point to pain, low mood or another need to report."),
("What does supporting a person's independence mean?",
 "Helping them do what they can", ["Doing tasks for them to save effort", "Leaving them alone most of the day", "Letting them do anything unsupported"],
 "Supporting independence means encouraging people to do as much as they can, with the right help, so they keep skills and confidence."),
("Which of these could show that a person is in pain but cannot say so?",
 "Grimacing and guarding one area", ["Asking for a cup of tea", "Chatting with other residents", "Sleeping at the usual time"],
 "Signs of pain include facial expressions, guarding, changes in posture, restlessness, withdrawal or new distress. Report them so pain can be assessed."),
("A person seems withdrawn and tearful. What should you do?",
 "Comfort them and report it", ["Leave them alone until it passes", "Tell them to try to cheer up", "Record it only if it lasts a month"],
 "Emotional distress matters as much as physical pain. Offer comfort, listen and report changes so their wellbeing can be supported."),
("How can you support a person's wellbeing?",
 "Help them keep up what matters", ["Keep their days the same as others'", "Limit visits to save disruption", "Discourage new hobbies"],
 "Wellbeing includes feeling valued, connected and able to do things that matter, such as faith, hobbies, relationships and routines."),
("What is a life history or one-page profile used for?",
 "Knowing the person's story", ["Recording their medicines", "Planning staff training", "Billing for care hours"],
 "Life story work and one-page profiles help staff understand what is important to the person and how best to support them."),
("You are about to help someone wash. What protects their privacy?",
 "Close curtains and cover them", ["Leave the door open for air", "Chat with colleagues in the room", "Wash them near the window"],
 "Closing doors and curtains and keeping parts of the body covered when not being washed protect privacy and dignity."),
("What should you do before entering a person's bedroom?",
 "Knock and wait for an answer", ["Walk straight in if the door is shut", "Knock as you open the door", "Only knock in the evening"],
 "A person's room is their private space. Knock and wait for an answer, or follow their agreed preference if they cannot answer."),
("What does dignity mean in care?",
 "Treating people as individuals", ["Giving care as fast as possible", "Using pet names to be friendly", "Doing everything for people"],
 "Dignity means treating people with respect, as individuals whose choices, privacy and feelings matter."),
("A person wants to be called by their title and surname. What should you do?",
 "Use the name they prefer", ["Use their first name to be friendly", "Call them \"love\" or \"dear\"", "Use whatever the team uses"],
 "Use the name the person chooses. Terms like \"love\" or \"dear\" can feel patronising and undermine dignity."),
("You need to discuss a person's continence care with a colleague. Where should you do it?",
 "Somewhere private", ["In the shared lounge", "Quietly in the corridor", "At the dining table"],
 "Personal information should be discussed where others cannot overhear, to protect privacy and confidentiality."),
("A person's family want them to stop seeing a friend from church. The person has capacity and wants to keep seeing them. What should happen?",
 "Support the person's choice", ["Follow the family's wishes", "Stop the visits for a trial", "Let the friend decide"],
 "A person with capacity has the right to decide who they see. Supporting relationships and community links is part of dignity and wellbeing."),
("How can you support a person to stay part of their community?",
 "Help them keep up local links", ["Plan all activities inside the home", "Choose new groups for them", "Limit trips to save staff time"],
 "Community links such as clubs, faith groups and friends support wellbeing and independence. Follow the person's choices."),
("When is it right to share information about a person's personal care with others?",
 "When they need to know to give care", ["When visitors ask how the person is", "When a neighbour is concerned", "When a colleague is curious"],
 "Share personal information only on a need-to-know basis, with people involved in the person's care, following agreed ways of working."),
("A person with mobility needs prefers to dress slowly by themselves. The morning is busy. What should you do?",
 "Give them time to dress themselves", ["Dress them to keep to schedule", "Ask them to stay in nightwear", "Dress them and record it as their choice"],
 "Taking over to save time removes independence and dignity. Support the person at their pace and raise staffing pressure with your manager."),
("Which of these is a way to respect a person's dignity at mealtimes?",
 "Offer choice and go at their pace", ["Feed them quickly to keep to time", "Put aprons on everyone at the table", "Stand over them while helping them eat"],
 "Offer choices, sit at the person's level if helping them eat, go at their pace and use protective clothing only if they want it."),
("What should you do if you see a colleague leave a person exposed during personal care?",
 "Cover the person and report it", ["Say nothing to avoid upset", "Wait until the end of the shift", "Mention it to the person's visitor"],
 "Protect the person straight away, then report it through agreed ways of working. Ignoring poor practice can allow it to continue."),
("What is meant by active participation?",
 "The person shares in their own care", ["Staff take charge of all the tasks", "Families make every decision", "The person joins a group exercise class"],
 "Active participation means the person is a partner in their care and daily life, not a passive recipient."),
("Which question best supports a person to make their own choice about clothes?",
 "Which would you like to wear?", ["You'll wear this one, won't you?", "Shall I just pick something?", "Why not wear yesterday's top?"],
 "Offering real options in a clear, open way supports choice. Leading questions or choosing for the person take control away."),
("Why should you not discuss one resident's care in front of another?",
 "It breaches their privacy", ["It wastes too much staff time", "It may create a fire risk", "It confuses the staff rota"],
 "Talking about someone's care where others can hear breaches their privacy and confidentiality and can cause embarrassment."),
]
